    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d387">

                <pb facs="0552" n="458" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg458"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">881.00/926: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Portugal</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dearing</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lisbon</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-06-06T18:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 6, 1924—6
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 7:35 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>31. Department’s 23, June 4, 5 p.m.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d387-f1"/> Tangier
                    convention. Confidentially informed by Foreign Office that Portuguese Government
                    will presently “ratify” the convention without conditions or reservations and
                    has same attitude toward port concession.</p>
                <p>Foreign Office states that Portugal found convention unsatisfactory and would
                    have preferred larger representation, at least two, but decided after careful
                    study that under existing circumstances unconditional adhesion was best. …</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dearing</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
